  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-x?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,2),(-2,-1)$ and $(2,-2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,2)}=1-(-2)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=1-(-2)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-2)}=1-2=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/911af9ca268f5f4c9cbac7b9c063eae54defce6d99dfadef609892b93ad58ae4.png)",
